  },
  "HostTemplate": "https://{accountId}.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com",
  "HostTemplateNote": "NetSuite uses per-account subdomains for all REST calls. The accountId is a per-connection runtime value (e.g. ''1234567'' for production or ''1234567_SB1'' for sandbox) supplied in the credential. The connector builds the base URL at runtime as ''https://{accountId}.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com''. This is NOT a baked-in constant. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide request examples throughout: GET https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/record/v1/customer (demo account ''demo123''). Postman tutorial notes: ''You can download the REST API Postman environment template and collection of sample requests from the SuiteTalk tools download page at https://{accountID}.app.netsuite.com/...''. Async job response Location header example: ''https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/async/v1/job/{id}''. Docs URL: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1540391670.html (Overview) and https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1544794192.html (Postman chapter).",
  "AuthFlow": "oauth1-tba",
  "AuthFlowNote": "NetSuite TBA (Token-Based Authentication) uses OAuth 1.0a-style four-part signing: ConsumerKey, ConsumerSecret, TokenID, TokenSecret — all from the Integration Record + Access Token. This is the currently implemented credential type (NetSuite TBA, ID A72945D2-9E38-429D-904D-F3ACCE17CBAB). The connector signs each request with an Authorization header containing OAuth realm, oauth_consumer_key, oauth_token, oauth_signature_method, oauth_timestamp, oauth_nonce, and oauth_signature. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide Setting Up Authentication page (https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/article_0627022005.html): ''NetSuite supports two mechanisms: OAuth 2.0 and token-based authentication (TBA), robust, industry standard-based mechanisms. These authentication mechanisms enable client applications to use a token to access NetSuite through APIs, eliminating the need for integrations to store user credentials. REST web services do not support login through user credentials.'' Also: ''Note: OAuth 2.0 is the preferred authentication method. You should consider using OAuth 2.0 instead of TBA whenever possible.''",
  "AuthFlowAlternate": "oauth2-authcode",
  "AuthFlowAlternateNote": "NetSuite also supports OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code flow. Requires creating an Integration Record and obtaining client_id + client_secret. Oracle states OAuth 2.0 is the PREFERRED method but is not the currently implemented credential type for this connector. Setting Up OAuth 2.0 Authentication for REST Web Services: ''To set up OAuth 2.0, see Getting Started with OAuth 2.0. To be able to use REST web services with OAuth 2.0, you''re required to create an application using an integration record.'' Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/article_0627022005.html",
  "AuthHeaderPattern": "Authorization: OAuth realm=\"{accountId}\", oauth_consumer_key=\"{ConsumerKey}\", oauth_token=\"{TokenID}\", oauth_signature_method=\"HMAC-SHA256\", oauth_timestamp=\"{ts}\", oauth_nonce=\"{nonce}\", oauth_version=\"1.0\", oauth_signature=\"{sig}\"",
  "AuthHeaderPatternNote": "NetSuite TBA uses OAuth 1.0a Authorization header with realm set to the account ID. The signature is HMAC-SHA256 over the base string per OAuth 1.0 spec. For OAuth 2.0, the pattern is the standard ''Authorization: Bearer <access_token>''. The connector implements TBA signing. Source: Implied from netsuite-tba.schema.json credential structure (ConsumerKey, ConsumerSecret, TokenID, TokenSecret) and OAuth 1.0a/TBA documentation. EvidenceStrength: ImpliedFromExample (the schema fields and NetSuite TBA documentation together imply this header shape).",
  "RecordServiceBasePath": "/services/rest/record/v1",
  "RecordServiceBasePathNote": "All CRUD operations on NetSuite records go through /services/rest/record/v1/{recordType} (list/create) and /services/rest/record/v1/{recordType}/{id} (read/update/delete/upsert). Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide, chapter ''Working with Records'' and examples throughout: GET https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/record/v1/customer, POST /services/rest/record/v1/salesorder, PATCH /services/rest/record/v1/salesorder/1, etc. Docs: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1540811107.html",
  "SuiteQLPath": "/services/rest/query/v1/suiteql",
  "SuiteQLPathNote": "SuiteQL queries (SQL-92-based) are executed via POST to /services/rest/query/v1/suiteql with a JSON body {q: ''<SQL query>''} and the required header Prefer: transient. Paging: ?limit=N&offset=N. Max 100,000 results via REST (use SuiteAnalytics Connect ODBC for more). Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide ''Executing SuiteQL Queries Through REST Web Services'' (https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/section_157909186990.html): ''You can execute SuiteQL queries through REST web services by sending a POST request to the suiteql resource... POST https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/query/v1/suiteql?limit=10 offset=10 Prefer: transient {\"q\": \"SELECT email, COUNT(*) as count FROM transaction GROUP BY email\"}''",
  "MetadataCatalogPath": "/services/rest/record/v1/metadata-catalog",
  "MetadataCatalogPathNote": "The metadata catalog endpoint lists all available record types (GET) or their schemas in OpenAPI 3.0 (Accept: application/swagger+json) or JSON Schema (Accept: application/schema+json). A per-record-type schema is at /services/rest/record/v1/metadata-catalog/{recordType}. The catalog is per-user and shows standard + custom records/fields. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide ''Getting Metadata'' (https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/section_1540810174.html): ''The endpoint for getting the metadata schema for all exposed records is http://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/record/v1/metadata-catalog ... GET http://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/record/v1/metadata-catalog''. Working with Resource Metadata: ''The metadata catalog defines all available records, their fields, available values (for example, for enum fields), sublists and subrecords (both standard and custom ones), and their various properties. As new customizations are added to the system, they appear in the metadata catalog as well.''",
  "DiscoveryIsAuthoritativeAssessment": "conditional-yes",
  "DiscoveryIsAuthoritativeNote": "The /services/rest/record/v1/metadata-catalog endpoint returns the complete gamut of records accessible to the authenticated user (standard + all custom). The docs state: ''As new customizations are added to the system, they appear in the metadata catalog as well.'' and ''This dynamic NetSuite model reflects the customizations and features enabled or disabled in your account.'' This implies the catalog IS authoritative for the credentialed user context — if a record type no longer appears it has been genuinely removed/disabled. However, this requires runtime verification with real credentials (credential-gated) before committing DiscoveryIsAuthoritative=true. Gate: true if the connector''s DiscoverObjects returns a non-zero count that matches what the account admin sees in Setup > Records Catalog. Source: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/section_1540810174.html + https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/article_159367781370.html.",
  "APIVersioningStrategy": "url-path",
  "APIVersioningStrategyNote": "API version is embedded in the URL path as /services/rest/record/v1/ and /services/rest/query/v1/. Currently at version v1 throughout. Source: All URL examples in SuiteTalk REST API Guide use /v1/ in paths. E.g.: GET https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/record/v1/customer; POST https://demo123.suitetalk.api.netsuite.com/services/rest/query/v1/suiteql. Docs: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1540391670.html",
  "PaginationDefaults": {
