  "CustomObjectMarkerPattern": "customrecord_{internalId}",
  "CustomObjectMarkerPatternNote": "Custom record types in NetSuite are accessible via the REST API as ''customrecord_{scriptId}'' (the script ID assigned when the record was created, e.g. ''customrecord_project_task''). Custom records appear in the metadata catalog the same as standard records. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide ''Working with Resource Metadata'': ''Custom record metadata is accessible the same way as metadata for standard records. As new customizations are added to the system, they appear in the metadata catalog as well.'' and ''Working with Records'': ''Using CRUD Operations on Custom Records, Custom Lists, and Custom Transactions v1''. EvidenceStrength: ImpliedFromExample (the pattern ''customrecord_'' is the standard NetSuite script ID prefix for custom records).",
  "CustomFieldMarkerPattern": "custentity_{scriptId} / custbody_{scriptId} / custcolumn_{scriptId} / custitem_{scriptId}",
  "CustomFieldMarkerPatternNote": "Custom fields in NetSuite are identified by their scriptId prefix based on type: custentity_ (entity custom fields on Customer, Contact, etc.), custbody_ (transaction body fields), custcolumn_ (transaction line item fields), custitem_ (item custom fields). In JSON Schema metadata, custom fields carry ''x-ns-custom-field: true''. Source: Working with JSON Schema Metadata example (https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/section_1545129726.html): ''\"custentity_integer\": { \"title\": \"Integer Number\", \"type\": \"integer\", ..., \"x-ns-custom-field\": true }''. EvidenceStrength: ImpliedFromExample.",
  "WriteCapability": {
    "Create": true,
    "Update": true,
    "Delete": true,
    "note": "Full CRUD is supported for all record types that support the respective operations. The metadata catalog (OpenAPI 3.0) documents which HTTP methods (GET, POST, PATCH, DELETE) are available per record type. PATCH is used for partial updates. An upsert (PATCH with externalId) is also supported. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide ''Working with Records'': ''Using REST web services, you can perform CRUD (create, read, update, delete) operations on NetSuite records.'' and chapter TOC listing ''Creating a Record Instance'', ''Getting a Record Instance'', ''Updating a Record Instance'', ''Using the Upsert Operation'', ''Deleting a Record Instance''. URL: https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1540811107.html"
  },
  "ConcurrencyControl": "none",
  "ConcurrencyControlNote": "NetSuite REST Web Services does not document ETags, If-Match headers, or version fields for optimistic concurrency control on individual record operations. PATCH operations update the record without conditional headers. The docuement mentions ''Using the Upsert Operation'' but no concurrency mechanism. Conflict resolution must use application-level snapshot comparison (MostRecent / DestWins logic). EvidenceStrength: ImpliedFromExample (ETags/If-Match are absent from all documented request/response examples and the error code list).",
  "DeleteSemantics": "hard",
  "DeleteSemanticsNote": "DELETE on /services/rest/record/v1/{recordType}/{id} permanently removes the record. NetSuite does have an ''isInactive'' boolean field on many record types for logical deactivation (equivalent to a soft delete at the application level), but the REST DELETE verb performs hard deletion. The connector must handle both scenarios: records soft-deleted via isInactive=true will still appear in SuiteQL queries unless filtered. Source: SuiteTalk REST API Guide ''Deleting a Record Instance'' listed in the Working with Records chapter (https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/saas/netsuite/ns-online-help/chapter_1540811107.html). EvidenceStrength: ImpliedFromExample (DELETE is a standard REST verb listed without any archival-flag qualifier in the CRUD docs).",
  "DefaultSyncDirection": "Pull",
  "DefaultSyncDirectionNote": "Read-only (Pull) is the safe default for new EntityMaps. NetSuite is an ERP system where incorrect writes can affect accounting records, financial transactions, and multi-subsidiary operations. Bidirectional must be validated with the client against a sandbox account before enabling, and write operations tested per-record-type. The client must confirm which record types should be bidirectional.",
